  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A few showers and thunderstorms are possible \nfrom Central Virginia to Southern Maryland tonight. \n\n00Z surface analysis places a cold front from near Wilmington DE\nto Annapolis MD to Culpeper VA to Staunton VA. Moderate amounts\nof instability and flow remain along and south of this front,\nwhich is intersecting a northward-moving outflow boundary from\nearlier convection that impacted the Fredericksburg to southern\nMD area. This corridor may continue to see activity tonight, but\nwith decreasing severity especially beyond late evening as the\nfront continues to push south and diurnal instability wanes.\n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Below average temperatures and mainly dry\nconditions are expected Thursday through Saturday.\n\nA sprawling area of high pressure will build to our north for \nThursday and Friday, before shifting off the New England \nCoastline on Saturday. Northerly flow ahead of the approaching \nhigh will advect a much cooler and drier airmass into the region\nfor Thursday. Well below normal temperatures and dewpoints are \nforecast for late July, with high temperatures only in the upper\n70s/low 80s. Dewpoints in the 50s will also make it feel much \nless humid than is typical for July. Mostly sunny skies will \naccompany the well below normal temperatures/dewpoints. \nTemperatures will stay well below normal for Friday and Saturday\nwithin easterly flow. Some moisture could be drawn northward\nfrom the stalled frontal zone across the southeastern states\nduring this time. The current forecast has some low rain chances\nboth days, mainly in southern portions of the CWA and along the\nAllegheny Mountains.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... Gradual increase in moisture & precipitation \nchances early next week. \n\nBroad high pressure will continue to shift northeast over the \nweekend, with low pressure building over northeastern Canada \nand a stemming trough potentially forming over the Mid-Atlantic late \nSunday and into Monday. Dewpoints start to increase during this \ntime, so shower & thunderstorm chances could increase during the \nafternoon & evening hours Sunday and Monday as this perturbation \nmoves through. Machine-learning algorithms show some lower-end \nprobabilities for severe occurrence during this time, although \nensembles still show uncertainty in timing of the overall synoptic \nsetup. As this trough approaches, temperatures gradually begin to \nincrease back into the upper 80s.\n\nDewpoints could remain elevated within the region as this system \nmoves through, with the upper-level low to the north slowly \ntracking to the south before shifting to the northeast midweek. This \ncould overall re-introduce chances for rain within the region \nafter a brief dry spell.",
